#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541096 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064365 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9911dc0d-905d-54e1-aadb-f4fbce6f89d2" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating an Inverse Tangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "beda0c51-788b-57b5-9c1d-b837a9961c32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{t^2+25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540846}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "beda0c51-788b-57b5-9c1d-b837a9961c32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{t^2+25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540846} :answer-field/id #uuid "a16e017f-d9d9-54ce-97a3-a549b27da1ca" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541011}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6e20ee7e-741b-5069-a51c-7de25cc32da7" :question/math-academy-id "q-133285" :question/problem "Calculate the horizontal velocity of the eagle as a function of time.\n\nThe horizontal velocity of the bird is$\\,$ \n{{field-1}}$\\,\\text{m/s}.$" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal velocity of the eagle is given by ${x}^{′}(t),$ where \n\n$$\nx(t)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{t}{5}).\n$$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of arctangent is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t)=\\frac{1}{1+{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule and the formula above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{t}{5})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+(t/5)^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{t}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{t}^{2}/25}\\cdot \\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{25+{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the horizontal velocity of the eagle is given by the function ${x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{5}{25+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{m/s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541112 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138430 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a35a2fee-a1a5-59aa-9390-f73d2898320c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Constant of Variation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c18a0d99-6ac2-55bc-b565-e5db3275b58f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t^2-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540851}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c18a0d99-6ac2-55bc-b565-e5db3275b58f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t^2-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540851} :answer-field/id #uuid "26a95aab-3ec7-5d58-8faa-efd0ff8b9e68" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540960}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "95631ad4-7573-5741-9c54-4f854208188c" :question/math-academy-id "q-133286" :question/problem "The vertical velocity of the eagle increases at a rate proportional to the square of the time $t.$ In addition, the eagle loses $2\\,\\text{m/s}$ in the positive $y$-direction due to wind resistance. It is known that the eagle's vertical velocity equals $2\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=2\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nThe differential equation that describes the eagle's vertical velocity is ${y}^{′}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The vertical velocity increases proportionally with ${t}^{2}$ and decreases by $2.$ The differential equation that describes this is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(t)=k{t}^{2}-2,\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a (positive) constant of proportionality.\n\nSince ${y}^{′}(2)=2,$ we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(2) & =2 \\\\ k(2)^{2}-2 & =2 \\\\ 4k & =4 \\\\ k & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(t)={t}^{2}-2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541058 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083240 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8922ab07-5ac7-5f85-bfee-c30de7f4024a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72d5bd5e-7472-57dd-bbfd-f86c424a9071" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(t^2-2)(t^2+25)}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540725}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72d5bd5e-7472-57dd-bbfd-f86c424a9071" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(t^2-2)(t^2+25)}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540725} :answer-field/id #uuid "796d2edf-72cd-5edb-a250-8f0040849fed" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540991}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "08e75bed-6305-557a-ac6f-4a738a0fec12" :question/math-academy-id "q-133287" :question/problem "The eagle's path traces a curve $C$ in the $xy$-plane. Suppose that the eagle is at the point $P$ on $C$ at time $t.$ Expressed in terms of $t,$ what function gives the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P?$\n\nThe slope of the tangent to $C$ at time $t$ equals$\\,$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The slope of the tangent to $C$ equals $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$ To calculate this, we use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nIn parts 1 and 2, we found\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{5}{25+{t}^{2}},\\,{y}^{′}(t)={t}^{2}-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{t}^{2}-2}{(\\frac{5}{25+{t}^{2}})}=\\frac{1}{5}({t}^{2}-2)({t}^{2}+25).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541078 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083278 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fc81cba-b997-54bc-9e11-0a4ff130833e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points Where the Tangent is Horizontal or Vertical"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b291071b-8c33-51cd-93d8-3d6f97e2bee1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540826}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b291071b-8c33-51cd-93d8-3d6f97e2bee1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540826} :answer-field/id #uuid "d12141e2-6a38-52ff-846b-6b7417e1ae0f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541032}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4086d61d-9c18-5ba7-b425-e5509f913d48" :question/math-academy-id "q-133288" :question/problem "The tangent line to the eagle's flight path is horizontal at $t=${{field-1}}$\\,\\text{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nIn parts 1 and 2, we found\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{5}{25+{t}^{2}},\\,{y}^{′}(t)={t}^{2}-2.\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n{t}^{2}-2=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\pm \\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $-\\sqrt{2}\\notin (0,\\infty ),$ so we only consider the value $t=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\sqrt{2})=\\frac{5}{27}\\ne 0,\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the curve's tangent is horizontal when $t=\\sqrt{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541148 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175939 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f8f3989e-bde0-5b3a-8d45-08f34d330c3f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Intervals of Increasing or Decreasing Displacement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7bfa30b5-acb3-5f10-b07c-946951bce63e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540736}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7bfa30b5-acb3-5f10-b07c-946951bce63e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540736} :answer-field/id #uuid "7164cda6-7d07-5c84-a4dc-5817c12576d3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540988} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2e2f173-a72c-520b-adec-a73efbc497dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540853}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2e2f173-a72c-520b-adec-a73efbc497dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540853} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5bc5e64-6160-5d46-8ae4-099d090712d6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541028}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eb009f1b-54cb-513a-ae27-a2c22d910e4d" :question/math-academy-id "q-133289" :question/problem "The eagle is descending for all $t$ in the interval$\\,t\\in ($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" :question/worked-solution "If the eagle is descending, then its vertical velocity is negative.\n\nWe know that the eagle's vertical velocity is given by ${y}^{′}(t)={t}^{2}-2.$\n\nTo calculate the time interval in which the eagle's vertical velocity is decreasing, we solve the inequality ${y}^{′}(t)<0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & <0 \\\\ {t}^{2}-2 & <0 \\\\ {t}^{2} & <2 \\\\ |t| & <\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we know that $t>0.$ Therefore, the eagle is descending for $t\\in (0,\\sqrt{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541100 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078566 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b08650da-89e7-5a9d-89a6-de3623344662" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Value of a Function at a Point Using a Tangent Line Approximation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9cc6edc-3d58-5b7d-be26-011da007a10e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{80}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540814}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9cc6edc-3d58-5b7d-be26-011da007a10e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{80}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540814} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5800f9f-a331-5cd2-b8d9-dac2799c0d71" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541013}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "742d438b-e3dc-52b0-a068-888e70c6be15" :question/math-academy-id "q-133290" :question/problem "At time $t=5\\,\\text{s}$ the eagle is located at a point on $C$ with $y$-coordinate equal to $\\frac{95}{3}\\,\\text{m}.$ Use the tangent line approximation to approximate the $y$-coordinate of the eagle's location when $t=\\frac{110}{23}\\,\\text{s}.$\n\n$y(\\frac{110}{23})≈$\n{{field-1}}$\\,\\text{m}$" :question/worked-solution "The tangent line approximation to $y(t)$ at the point $a=5$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(t) & =y(a)+{y}^{′}(a)(t-a) \\\\  & =y(5)+{y}^{′}(5)(t-5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $y(5)=\\frac{95}{3},$ and since ${y}^{′}(t)={t}^{2}-2,$ then ${y}^{′}(5)=25-2=23.$ \n\nSo, the tangent line approximation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(t) & =\\frac{95}{3}+23(t-5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $y(\\frac{110}{23})$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=\\frac{110}{23}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\frac{110}{23}) & ≈L(\\frac{110}{23}) \\\\  & =\\frac{95}{3}+23(\\frac{110}{23}-5) \\\\  & =\\frac{95}{3}+23(-\\frac{5}{23}) \\\\  & =\\frac{95}{3}-5 \\\\  & =\\frac{80}{3}\\,\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541108 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184153 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0db9b1c3-1e31-53f6-9fc2-1c611e2f9398" :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Integral Using the Sum Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c412eada-9cf7-59a5-905e-0f6c0063eb13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{t^3}{3}-2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540856}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c412eada-9cf7-59a5-905e-0f6c0063eb13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{t^3}{3}-2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540856} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1f591a2-abaf-568b-95d2-b28ddb7d4b0c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541017}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "836e24be-b58e-500b-bb05-e9b2030ce5e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-133291" :question/problem "Find $y(t),$ the antiderivative of ${y}^{′}(t).$\n\n$y(t)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+\\,C$" :question/worked-solution "We know that ${y}^{′}(t)={t}^{2}-2.$\n\nApplying the sum rule, we compute the sum of the integrals of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {t}^{2}-2\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int {t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t-\\int 2\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int {t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t-2\\int {t}^{0}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{{t}^{2+1}}{2+1})-2t+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}-2t+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541063 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064196 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "67bbd172-1940-5a1e-82f8-ffc73058f009" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Definite Integral of a Polynomial Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88892d02-3412-5492-af0a-49ebe61d9b7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540755}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "88892d02-3412-5492-af0a-49ebe61d9b7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540755} :answer-field/id #uuid "70887428-4f7c-590f-a390-58a8f2bf8ee3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540987}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "143c70c1-44db-5544-97bc-c449974fa03d" :question/math-academy-id "q-133292" :question/problem "The eagle's total displacement in the $y$-direction over the first $6$ seconds is given by ${\\int }_{0}^{6}{y}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.$ Calculate this displacement.\n\nThe eagle's total displacement in the $y$-direction over the first $6$ seconds is$\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,\\text{m}.$" :question/worked-solution "In part 7, we found that the antiderivative of ${y}^{′}(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}-2t+C.\n$$\n\nBy the fundamental theorem of calculus, we take the antiderivative and evaluate the difference at the bounds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{6}{y}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =y(t){|}_{0}^{6} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}-2t)|}_{0}^{6} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}(6)^{3}-2(6))-(\\frac{1}{3}(0)^{3}-2(0)) \\\\  & =60-0 \\\\  & =60.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe eagle's total displacement in the $y$-direction over the first $6$ seconds is $60\\,\\text{m}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541151 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068858 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e520dc67-3b63-5ca7-959b-f642d85529d6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Magnitude of a Vector-Valued Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4c60dd5-4f2f-5247-b508-e4412be02a72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "31.68" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540888}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4c60dd5-4f2f-5247-b508-e4412be02a72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "31.68" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540888} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f220aba-10fa-5140-91ce-bb3c746dbdc3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541001}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f4a5e43e-e7a6-55b0-a709-f89ec3a86933" :question/math-academy-id "q-133293" :question/problem "Let $r(t)=⟨x(t),y(t)⟩$ be the position vector of the eagle. Find the distance between the eagle and its nest at time $t=5\\,\\text{s},$ rounded to two decimal places.\n\nThe eagle is approximately$\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{m}$ away from its nest after $t=5$ seconds." :question/worked-solution "Since the nest is at the origin, the distance between the bird and its nest at time $t=5$ is $|r(5)|.$\n\nWe know that $y(5)=\\frac{95}{3}.$ Evaluating the function at $t=5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(5) & =⟨\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{5}{5}),\\frac{95}{3}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{95}{3}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the magnitude of this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|r(5)| & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}+{(\\frac{95}{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{16}+\\frac{9\\,025}{9}} \\\\  & ≈31.68\\,\\text{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541128 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198355 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "072ad553-dd0b-58e0-b3e2-082c6d9866dd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Vector-Valued Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c621d975-7e42-532e-8cd1-a5a95ff3741c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540860}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c621d975-7e42-532e-8cd1-a5a95ff3741c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540860} :answer-field/id #uuid "280a3379-43b0-5e7e-835c-37f28ffa4d1a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540961} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cdc34de8-c927-5f9c-8dbf-b5874f7e2681" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540873}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cdc34de8-c927-5f9c-8dbf-b5874f7e2681" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540873} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d783132-2979-54b3-9fd1-5a7588deaa6d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540978}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c1baae25-37ff-504a-9b40-31c45e15f352" :question/math-academy-id "q-133294" :question/problem "${r}^{′}(\\sqrt{5})=\\,$$⟨$\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$⟩$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate each component separately.\n\n* For the $x$-component, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{5}{25+{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand therefore,\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\sqrt{5})=\\frac{5}{25+(\\sqrt{5})^{2}}=\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\n* For the $y$-component, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(t)={t}^{2}-2,\n$$\n\nand therefore,\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(\\sqrt{5})=(\\sqrt{5})^{2}-2=3.\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\sqrt{5}) & =⟨{x}^{′}(\\sqrt{5}),{y}^{′}(\\sqrt{5})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{6},3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
